{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FcqWDGt5sSmx6LuPf7E11N","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"ACL ARR 2026 reviewer guidelines scoring rubric scale\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"ARR Reviewer Guidelines – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewerguidelines\"},{\"title\":\"ARR Area Chair Guidelines – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/acguidelines\"},{\"title\":\"Development and Validation of a Scoring Rubric for Editorial Evaluation of Peer-review Quality: A Pilot Study\",\"url\":\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC11000557/\"},{\"title\":\"ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/\"},{\"title\":\"Authors Guidelines – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/authors\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found information about ACL ARR 2026 reviewer guidelines:\n\nStarting with February 2025, ARR reviewers are asked to provide three scores: soundness, excitement, and overall recommendation. The soundness scores must be justified by the text of the review.\n\nThe search results from the official ARR Reviewer Guidelines page provide information about the scoring approach, though the detailed scoring rubric scale values are not shown in the excerpt retrieved. The results indicate that the guidelines address various aspects of the review process including managing bias and ensuring consistency in evaluation, but the specific numerical scale for each score metric is not included in these search results.\n\nFor the complete detailed rubric with specific scale values, you may want to visit the full ARR Reviewer Guidelines page directly at http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewerguidelines.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d76881f3-8cf2-45f7-8657-e72190e5783a","uuid":"1c62978b-f46b-4cd5-a616-340d5b5cbe6f","timestamp":"2026-05-22T03:10:45.205Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"ACL ARR 2026 reviewer guidelines scoring rubric scale","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01Wga6gyLUeV6AkerwkXx94E","content":[{"title":"ARR Reviewer Guidelines – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewerguidelines"},{"title":"ARR Area Chair Guidelines – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/acguidelines"},{"title":"Development and Validation of a Scoring Rubric for Editorial Evaluation of Peer-review Quality: A Pilot Study","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC11000557/"},{"title":"ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/"},{"title":"Authors Guidelines – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/authors"}]},"Based on the search results, I found information about ACL ARR 2026 reviewer guidelines:\n\nStarting with February 2025, ARR reviewers are asked to provide three scores: soundness, excitement, and overall recommendation. The soundness scores must be justified by the text of the review.\n\nThe search results from the official ARR Reviewer Guidelines page provide information about the scoring approach, though the detailed scoring rubric scale values are not shown in the excerpt retrieved. The results indicate that the guidelines address various aspects of the review process including managing bias and ensuring consistency in evaluation, but the specific numerical scale for each score metric is not included in these search results.\n\nFor the complete detailed rubric with specific scale values, you may want to visit the full ARR Reviewer Guidelines page directly at http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewerguidelines."],"durationSeconds":4.918117323000002}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014HifdALxQ5QDgiwM9g9Kvr","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"ACL Rolling Review reviewer form 2025 2026 overall assessment soundness excitement\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Review Form – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewform\"},{\"title\":\"ARR Reviewer Guidelines – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewerguidelines\"},{\"title\":\"ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/\"},{\"title\":\"ARR Area Chair Guidelines – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/acguidelines\"},{\"title\":\"Authors Guidelines – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/authors\"},{\"title\":\"aclrollingreview/reviewerguidelines.md at main · acl-org/aclrollingreview\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/acl-org/aclrollingreview/blob/main/reviewerguidelines.md\"},{\"title\":\"ARR Changelog – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/changelog\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found information about the ACL Rolling Review (ARR) reviewer form for 2025-2026:\n\n## Three-Score Review System\n\nStarting with February 2025, ARR reviewers are asked to provide three scores: soundness, excitement, and overall recommendation.\n\n## Soundness Assessment\n\nReviewers evaluate whether the paper is sufficiently sound and thorough, considering whether it clearly states scientific claims and provides adequate support for them. The soundness scores must be justified by the text of the review.\n\n## Excitement Score\n\nExcitement is subjective and does not necessarily follow what is popular in the field, as papers may be perceived as transformational/innovative/surprising because they present conceptual breakthroughs or evidence challenging common assumptions/methods/datasets/metrics. Subjective preferences should be reflected in a separate excitement score, which is orthogonal to the assessment of soundness, and excitement scores reflect personal taste without necessarily coming with explicit reasons.\n\n## Overall Assessment\n\nThe Overall assessment score is an explicit recommendation for the outcome of the paper if it were committed to an *ACL venue, reflecting your assessment of soundness, excitement, and also other factors like novelty and impact.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d76881f3-8cf2-45f7-8657-e72190e5783a","uuid":"171b4ef6-8958-465d-8606-5bed7611d284","timestamp":"2026-05-22T03:10:47.919Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"ACL Rolling Review reviewer form 2025 2026 overall assessment soundness excitement","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01Ps9Q7QUU6qTLmLtRN8ccge","content":[{"title":"Review Form – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewform"},{"title":"ARR Reviewer Guidelines – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewerguidelines"},{"title":"ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/"},{"title":"ARR Area Chair Guidelines – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/acguidelines"},{"title":"Authors Guidelines – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/authors"},{"title":"aclrollingreview/reviewerguidelines.md at main · acl-org/aclrollingreview","url":"https://github.com/acl-org/aclrollingreview/blob/main/reviewerguidelines.md"},{"title":"ARR Changelog – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/changelog"}]},"Based on the search results, I found information about the ACL Rolling Review (ARR) reviewer form for 2025-2026:\n\n## Three-Score Review System\n\nStarting with February 2025, ARR reviewers are asked to provide three scores: soundness, excitement, and overall recommendation.\n\n## Soundness Assessment\n\nReviewers evaluate whether the paper is sufficiently sound and thorough, considering whether it clearly states scientific claims and provides adequate support for them. The soundness scores must be justified by the text of the review.\n\n## Excitement Score\n\nExcitement is subjective and does not necessarily follow what is popular in the field, as papers may be perceived as transformational/innovative/surprising because they present conceptual breakthroughs or evidence challenging common assumptions/methods/datasets/metrics. Subjective preferences should be reflected in a separate excitement score, which is orthogonal to the assessment of soundness, and excitement scores reflect personal taste without necessarily coming with explicit reasons.\n\n## Overall Assessment\n\nThe Overall assessment score is an explicit recommendation for the outcome of the paper if it were committed to an *ACL venue, reflecting your assessment of soundness, excitement, and also other factors like novelty and impact."],"durationSeconds":6.958524391999999}}
