{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01S6L7EToSJNgdcQkE4AzqEf","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have a thorough understanding of the paper, the experimental infrastructure, the actual completed experiments vs. imagined experiments, and the known issues. Let me compile the review.\n\n---\n\n## ACL ARR Review -- Reviewer 1 (R1): Methods and Experiments\n\n### Paper: \"Cross-Lingual Conflict Detection in Multilingual LLMs: Mechanistic Evidence for English-Anchored Representations\"\n\n---\n\n### Summary\n\nThis paper investigates how cross-lingual knowledge conflict signals are encoded in the residual stream of multilingual transformers. Using three analysis tracks -- linear/MLP probing (Track 1), SAE feature decomposition (Track 2), and causal representation steering (Track 3) -- the authors analyze three dense transformer architectures (Llama-3.1-8B, Qwen3-8B, Gemma-2-9B-IT) across two language pairs (EN--ZH, EN--JA). The central claim is that conflict detection is encoded in a language-universal mid-layer representation anchored to English, with language-specific processing emerging during late-layer transition. Evidence includes: 70--77% within-language probe accuracy, a consistent 6.3--7.9pp directional asymmetry favoring X->EN over EN->X transfer, SAE features showing mid-layer cross-lingual sharing (Jaccard=0.28) that decays in late layers (Jaccard=0.12), and steering flip rates of 17--35% that reproduce the same asymmetry pattern.\n\n---\n\n### Strengths\n\n**S1. Well-structured hypothesis-driven design.** The H1 vs. H2 framing is commendable. Rather than reporting a collection of exploratory findings, the paper derives specific, falsifiable predictions from each hypothesis (mid-layer Jaccard > late-layer Jaccard for H1; directional consistency across language pairs) and systematically tests them. This is good scientific practice rarely seen in interpretability papers.\n\n**S2. Three-track converging evidence.** The detection (probing) -> decomposition (SAE) -> intervention (steering) pipeline provides increasingly strong evidence for the claims. The steering experiments are particularly valuable because they move beyond correlational probing to causal manipulation. The fact that steering flip rate asymmetry mirrors the probe transfer asymmetry (Section 4.5, Tables 6 and 7) is the paper's strongest methodological contribution.\n\n**S3. Systematic confound controls.** The COMET tercile ablation (Table 1) is well-designed: rather than applying an arbitrary COMET threshold, the authors empirically test whether translation quality correlates with probe accuracy and find a null/reversed relationship. The token overlap analysis (Jaccard 3-4%, negative correlation in Llama) is similarly careful. The label noise ablation establishes safety margins relative to the verification pipeline's estimated error rate.\n\n**S4. Cross-architecture consistency.** Testing three architectures and finding a coefficient of variation below 6% for the asymmetry gap (7.2/7.2/7.9pp) is notable. The MLP non-linearity gradient anti-correlating with Chinese pre-training proportion (Table 3: Qwen +2.3pp, Llama +4.7pp, Gemma +6.1pp) is a clean finding that connects model training characteristics to mechanistic behavior.\n\n**S5. Appropriate statistical methodology.** Permutation tests (n=1000) are appropriate for the non-parametric setting. The use of balanced accuracy rather than raw accuracy is correct given the potential for class imbalance. The 5-fold stratified cross-validation is standard and appropriate.\n\n---\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n**W1 (Major). The paper is a \"dream report\" -- all experimental results beyond the MVP are imagined, not executed.**\n\nThis is the single most critical issue. Cross-referencing the paper's tables against the project's actual experimental state reveals a fundamental disconnect:\n\n- The ROADMAP.md shows only 2 experiments completed (`mvp_probe_v1`, `mvp_probe_llama_v1`), both on EN--ZH only, both on Llama-3.1-8B and Qwen3-8B only.\n- The experiment_plan.yaml shows all Post-MVP experiments (plan_102 through plan_303) in `planned` status, not `done`.\n- The `review_context.yaml` and `imagined_experiments.yaml` files explicitly describe these as imagined results in a \"best tier\" scenario.\n- The Gemma-2-9B-IT results (Table 2, row 3) are imagined -- the model download was interrupted at 11% (mistakes.yaml M010).\n- All EN--JA results (Table 4) are imagined -- plan_103 is `planned`.\n- All SAE results (Tables 5, 6, 7, 8, 9) are imagined -- plan_201/202/203/301/302/303 are all `planned`.\n- All steering results (Tables 6, 7, 8) are imagined -- plan_107/301/302 are all `planned`.\n- The CKA/SVCCA results (Table 5) are imagined -- plan_104 is `planned`.\n- The larger dataset validation (Table 10) is imagined -- plan_108 is `planned`.\n- Even the COMET results for Llama are still running (`comet_llama_v6` in report.md).\n\nOnly the core 2-model EN--ZH probe results and the Qwen COMET tercile ablation have been actually executed. Everything else in this paper is speculative. **As a reviewer, I cannot evaluate the soundness of experiments that have not been run.** The numbers could turn out quite differently in practice. The paper should be resubmitted when the experiments are actually completed.\n\n**W2 (Major). Bootstrap n=100 is insufficient for reliable 95% CIs.**\n\nThe standard recommendation for bootstrap CIs is n >= 1000 (Efron & Tibshirani, 1993; DiCiccio & Efron, 1996). At n=100, the 2.5th and 97.5th percentiles of the bootstrap distribution correspond to the 2nd and 3rd most extreme values, making CI endpoints highly sensitive to individual bootstrap samples. This is particularly concerning because:\n\n- The Qwen CI at N~1000 brackets zero ([-1.2, 13.4]), and the paper interprets this as \"trending\" rather than non-significant. With n=100 bootstrap, the lower CI bound could easily shift by 2-3pp either way.\n- The paper claims \"98/100 iterations positive\" for Llama and \"96/100 iterations positive\" for Qwen. These counts have binomial SE of ~1.4% and ~2.0% respectively, which is non-negligible.\n- The asymmetry gap is the paper's central quantitative claim. Using only 100 bootstrap iterations for the key statistical test undermines confidence in the precise CI bounds reported.\n\nThe authors should increase to n >= 2000 bootstrap iterations. This is computationally cheap given that each iteration only retrains a logistic regression probe.\n\n**W3 (Major). SAE analysis on base model vs. probing on instruct-tuned models.**\n\nThe paper applies Qwen-Scope SAEs (trained on Qwen3-8B-Base) for Track 2 while using instruct-tuned models for Track 1 probing. The paper acknowledges this (Limitations paragraph 3) but dismisses it too quickly with \"cross-validation between tracks partially mitigates.\" This mismatch is more serious than acknowledged because:\n\n- RLHF/instruction tuning substantially reorganizes representations, particularly in layers where the paper claims critical conflict processing occurs (mid-to-late layers). Anthropic's own work on monosemanticity notes that SAE features identified in base models may not correspond to the same features in fine-tuned versions.\n- The Jaccard analysis (Table 7) measures feature overlap in the SAE's native feature space on the base model. Applying these same features to explain instruct-model probe behavior is a logical gap.\n- The steering experiments (Tables 6-9) -- if they were actually run -- would need to be performed on the same model the SAE was trained on (base) OR the paper needs to demonstrate that SAE features transfer meaningfully to the instruct model. Neither is done.\n\n**W4 (Moderate). Probe accuracy of 70-77% is substantially below prior work and limits interpretive claims.**\n\nBoer et al. (2026) achieve 0.96 Macro-F1 for monolingual conflict attribution probing. The 70-77% balanced accuracy reported here represents a meaningful gap. The paper does not adequately explain this gap. Possible explanations include: (a) the cross-lingual setting is inherently harder, (b) the ConflictQA verification pipeline introduces noise, (c) the conflict signal in these models is genuinely weaker. The paper should distinguish between these possibilities because they have different implications for the strength of claims. If (c), the \"conflict detection is encoded in a language-universal representation\" claim is only partially supported -- it may be that only a fraction of conflict information is language-universal while a large portion is encoded in ways the linear probe cannot capture.\n\n**W5 (Moderate). Steering captures only 17-35% of samples, leaving the majority of the mechanism unexplained.**\n\nThe flip rates of 17-35% mean that 65-83% of samples are not affected by the intervention. While the paper correctly notes these rates are 3.4-6.5x above random baseline, the absolute magnitude raises questions about what fraction of the conflict resolution mechanism is captured by the linear/sparse approximation. The paper would benefit from an analysis of *which* samples flip and which do not -- are the non-flipping samples systematically different (e.g., higher confidence, particular topic domains)?\n\n**W6 (Moderate). Only two language pairs, both Asian, tested.**\n\nBoth EN--ZH and EN--JA are CJK languages with shared cultural context. The \"English-anchored\" hypothesis would be much more convincing with at least one typologically distant Indo-European language (e.g., EN--DE or EN--FR) where the English anchoring might behave differently due to shared script and morphological similarity. The paper's own framing references CLEAR's 10-language evaluation, making the 2-language coverage feel thin.\n\n**W7 (Minor). No train/test accuracy gap analysis for MLP probes.**\n\nThe paper notes MLP probes achieve +2.3 to +6.1pp gains in the weak transfer direction (Table 3) but does not report train accuracy for MLP probes. With a single hidden layer of 256 units operating on ~1000 samples with dimensionality 3584-4096, there is a real risk of overfitting. The paper states \"MLP probes (single hidden layer, 256 units) are trained with identical splits to quantify non-linear components\" but does not mention regularization (dropout, weight decay). A train/test accuracy comparison is needed to rule out that the MLP gain is simply memorization.\n\n**W8 (Minor). The Jaccard permutation test p=0.008 is based on very small feature counts.**\n\nThe mid-layer Jaccard of 0.28 comes from 8 shared features out of 21 EN and 18 ZH features (Table 7). The late-layer Jaccard of 0.12 comes from 3 shared features out of 14 EN and 10 ZH features. With such small counts, a single feature being reclassified between layers could meaningfully shift the Jaccard index. While p=0.008 passes conventional thresholds, the practical robustness of this finding to feature selection thresholds should be tested via sensitivity analysis.\n\n---\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n**Q1.** Can you confirm which experimental results in the paper have been actually executed vs. projected from the \"dream report\" pipeline? The discrepancy between the experiment tracking system and the paper is concerning.\n\n**Q2.** Why was bootstrap n set to 100 rather than the conventional 1000+? Was this a computational constraint? Given that each bootstrap iteration is a logistic regression fit, this seems unnecessarily conservative.\n\n**Q3.** For the COMET tercile ablation (Table 1), the Qwen tercile sizes are only 56 samples each. Have you computed the statistical power of Fisher's exact test at this sample size to detect a meaningful accuracy difference (say, 10pp)?\n\n**Q4.** The paper reports \"coherence is preserved at 91%\" for probe-based steering (Section 4.5). How is coherence measured? Is this human evaluation, automated metric, or perplexity-based?\n\n**Q5.** In the feature-set ablation (Table 9), \"shared features alone achieve 28.0% cross-lingual flip rate\" vs. EN-only 11.0% and ZH-only 9.0%. Have you verified that this difference is not simply due to shared features being at more causally effective layers (mid-layers) while language-specific features skew late (Table 6)?\n\n**Q6.** The MLP non-linearity gradient (Qwen 2.3pp < Llama 4.7pp < Gemma 6.1pp) is interpreted as anti-correlating with Chinese pre-training proportion. But Table 3 shows Gemma at 6.1pp, which is *higher* than Llama at 4.7pp. Yet Gemma is from Google (strong multilingual training). What evidence do you have about the relative Chinese data proportions in these three models' training sets?\n\n**Q7.** The token overlap analysis found Llama has a *negative* correlation (r=-0.15, p=0.012) between token overlap and probe accuracy. This is unexpected and warrants explanation. Why would higher token overlap *reduce* accuracy?\n\n---\n\n### Minor Issues\n\n1. **Table 2**: \"Sig.\" column uses \"Yes\"/\"No (trend)\" -- a binary significance judgment is more appropriate for a methods paper. The \"trend\" language invites selective interpretation. Report the CI and let readers judge.\n\n2. **Section 3.1**: \"We obtain model responses in English (EN) and generate high-quality translations in Chinese (ZH) and Japanese (JA) using the target models themselves.\" Using the same model for translation and as the probe target introduces a potential circularity -- the model's own biases in translation could systematically affect the conflict patterns in the translated text.\n\n3. **References**: Only 8 references cited in the paper. For an ACL submission, this is thin. Key missing references include: the original ConflictQA paper's full citation (Xie et al., 2024 is cited but only in references, not in the methods section where the dataset is described), Belinkov (2022) for probing methodology best practices, and Raghu et al. (2017) for SVCCA.\n\n4. **Reproducibility**: The paper does not mention random seeds, hyperparameter selection for L2 regularization in logistic regression, or the steering alpha search range/granularity.\n\n5. **Section 4.3**: \"The probe accuracy peak correlates negatively with the CKA transition point (r = -0.71)\" -- with only 3 data points (one per model), this correlation is not meaningful and should not be reported as if it were a statistical finding.\n\n6. The paper uses \"balanced accuracy\" throughout but the actual implementation in `train_probe.py` uses `clf.score()`, which returns raw accuracy, not balanced accuracy. This is a discrepancy between the paper's claims and the code.\n\n---\n\n### Preliminary Scores\n\n- **Soundness**: 2/5 -- The fundamental issue is that the vast majority of reported results are imagined rather than actually executed. The statistical methodology for the actually-completed experiments is generally appropriate but has the n=100 bootstrap weakness. The base/instruct SAE mismatch is inadequately addressed.\n\n- **Overall Assessment**: 2.0/5 (Reject) -- The paper presents an ambitious and well-structured research program with a clean hypothesis-driven design. However, the results as presented are largely speculative projections from a \"dream report\" pipeline rather than actual experimental outcomes. Only the 2-model EN--ZH probe experiments and partial COMET validation have been completed. This paper should be resubmitted once the experiments are actually executed, as the results could differ substantially from projections. If all experiments were genuinely completed and the numbers held, this would be a solid 3.5-4.0 paper. The three-track converging evidence design is strong, the confound controls are thorough, and the hypothesis framework is clean. But science requires actual data.\n\n**Confidence**: 4/5 -- I have high confidence in this assessment. I cross-referenced the paper's claims against the project's experiment tracking system, source code, and decision logs, which clearly distinguish completed from planned experiments.\n\n---\n\n### Actionable Recommendations for Revision\n\n1. Execute all planned experiments and resubmit with actual results.\n2. Increase bootstrap iterations to n >= 2000.\n3. Address the base/instruct SAE mismatch by either running SAE analysis on instruct models or providing transfer validation.\n4. Add at least one non-CJK language pair (e.g., EN--DE).\n5. Report MLP probe train/test accuracy gaps to rule out overfitting.\n6. Fix the `clf.score()` vs. balanced accuracy discrepancy in the code.\n7. Conduct sensitivity analysis on the SAE feature selection threshold and its impact on Jaccard indices."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: ac3a661244be2fc26 (use SendMessage with to: 'ac3a661244be2fc26' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 100447\ntool_uses: 22\nduration_ms: 157803</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d76881f3-8cf2-45f7-8657-e72190e5783a","uuid":"5f930258-a66f-466e-a0db-89c4300ac645","timestamp":"2026-05-22T03:14:44.065Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for an ACL ARR submission. Your focus is **Methods and Experiments**. \n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"Cross-Lingual Conflict Detection in Multilingual LLMs: Mechanistic Evidence for English-Anchored Representations\"\n\nThis paper investigates how cross-lingual knowledge conflict signals are encoded in the residual stream of multilingual transformers. It uses three analysis tracks:\n- Track 1: Linear and MLP probing on residual stream activations across 3 models (Llama-3.1-8B, Qwen3-8B, Gemma-2-9B-IT) and 2 language pairs (EN↔ZH, EN↔JA)\n- Track 2: SAE (Sparse Autoencoder) feature analysis using Qwen-Scope on Qwen3-8B-Base\n- Track 3: Causal intervention via probe-based and SAE-based representation steering\n\nKey findings:\n1. Linear probes detect conflict signals at 70-77% balanced accuracy (within-language), 64-73% (cross-lingual), all permutation test p<0.001\n2. Directional asymmetry: X→EN outperforms EN→X by 6.3-7.9pp across all 4 model×lang-pair combos (3/4 statistically significant at 95% CI, 4/4 directionally consistent)\n3. SAE finds 18 cross-lingually shared conflict features (mid-layer Jaccard=0.28, late-layer=0.12, p=0.008)\n4. Steering flip rates: monolingual EN 31.2%, cross-lingual ZH→EN 24.3%, EN→ZH 17.8%, all 3.4-6.5x above random baseline (~5%)\n5. Feature-set ablation: shared features alone achieve 28% flip rate vs language-specific 9-11%\n\nAdditional robustness:\n- COMET tercile ablation rules out translation quality confound\n- Label noise ablation: graceful degradation ~2.6pp per 5% noise\n- Larger dataset (N=2200) narrows Qwen CI from [-1.2, 13.4] to [1.8, 12.3], achieving significance\n- CKA/SVCCA shows mid-layer similarity ~0.82 dropping to ~0.54 in late layers\n- PCA visualization: mid-layer cross-lingual overlap (cosine=0.78), late-layer divergence (cosine=0.51)\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (from review_context.yaml)\nThe full imagined experiment list includes:\n1. COMET translation quality verification (mvp_003) — included in paper ✓\n2. Third model architecture (Gemma-2-9B-IT, plan_102) — included ✓\n3. Multilingual extension EN↔JA (plan_103) — included ✓\n4. Layer-wise CKA/SVCCA (plan_104) — included ✓\n5. Probe weight PCA visualization (plan_109) — included ✓\n6. Label noise ablation (plan_106) — included ✓\n7. Representation steering (plan_107) — included ✓\n8. Larger dataset validation (plan_108) — included ✓\n9. Qwen-Scope SAE conflict feature identification (plan_201) — included ✓\n10. LangFIR language feature deconfounding (plan_202) — included ✓\n11. Cross-lingual feature Jaccard analysis (plan_203) — included ✓\n12. Monolingual→cross-lingual steering transfer (plan_301) — included ✓\n13. Cross-lingual-specific steering (plan_302) — included ✓\n14. Typological steering correlation (plan_303) — included ✓ (but only 2 language pairs)\n\nAll 14 experiments appear to be covered in the paper.\n\n## Your Task\nEvaluate the paper's **methodology and experimental design** rigorously. Address:\n\n1. **Methodological Soundness**\n   - Is the probing methodology (logistic regression, 5-fold CV, balanced accuracy) appropriate?\n   - Is the bootstrap CI approach (n=100) for asymmetry gap sound? Is n=100 sufficient?\n   - Are permutation tests (n=1000) appropriate for significance testing?\n   - Is the MLP probe design (single hidden layer, 256 units) well-justified?\n   - Are the SAE methods (mutual information screening, Jaccard analysis, LangFIR) sound?\n   - Is the steering methodology (adding α·v to residual stream) well-controlled?\n\n2. **Experimental Coverage and Controls**\n   - Are all necessary controls present (random baselines, confound checks)?\n   - Is the COMET tercile ablation sufficient to rule out translation quality?\n   - Is the label noise ablation informative?\n   - Does the larger dataset validation adequately address the Qwen power issue?\n\n3. **Statistical Rigor**\n   - Qwen EN↔ZH asymmetry: CI includes zero at N=1000 but excludes zero at N=2200. Is this pattern concerning or expected?\n   - 3/4 significant, 4/4 directionally consistent — is this framing appropriate?\n   - Bootstrap n=100 — is this enough for reliable 95% CIs?\n   - The Jaccard permutation test p=0.008 — how robust is this given the small feature counts?\n\n4. **Potential Weaknesses**\n   - SAE analysis only on Qwen3-8B-Base (not on Llama or Gemma) — is this a significant limitation?\n   - SAE on base model vs probing on instruct-tuned models — how problematic is this mismatch?\n   - Probe accuracy 70-77% — what does this mean for the strength of claims?\n   - Steering flip rates 17-35% — what fraction of the mechanism is captured?\n   - Only dense transformers tested (no MoE) — how limiting?\n   - Only 2 Asian language pairs — no European languages tested\n\n5. **Missing Experiments or Analyses**\n   - Anything that should have been included but wasn't?\n   - Any ablations that would strengthen the claims?\n\n## Scoring (ACL ARR 1-5 scale)\nProvide preliminary scores:\n- **Soundness** (1-5): 5=most thorough given type, 4=sufficient support for all claims, 3=sufficient for main claims, 2=important issues, 1=fundamental flaws\n- **Overall Assessment** (1-5): 5=strong accept, 4=accept, 3.5=borderline accept, 3=borderline, 2.5=borderline reject, 2=reject\n\nFormat your review as a structured reviewer report with sections: Summary, Strengths, Weaknesses, Questions for Authors, Minor Issues, Preliminary Scores.","agentId":"ac3a661244be2fc26","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have a thorough understanding of the paper, the experimental infrastructure, the actual completed experiments vs. imagined experiments, and the known issues. Let me compile the review.\n\n---\n\n## ACL ARR Review -- Reviewer 1 (R1): Methods and Experiments\n\n### Paper: \"Cross-Lingual Conflict Detection in Multilingual LLMs: Mechanistic Evidence for English-Anchored Representations\"\n\n---\n\n### Summary\n\nThis paper investigates how cross-lingual knowledge conflict signals are encoded in the residual stream of multilingual transformers. Using three analysis tracks -- linear/MLP probing (Track 1), SAE feature decomposition (Track 2), and causal representation steering (Track 3) -- the authors analyze three dense transformer architectures (Llama-3.1-8B, Qwen3-8B, Gemma-2-9B-IT) across two language pairs (EN--ZH, EN--JA). The central claim is that conflict detection is encoded in a language-universal mid-layer representation anchored to English, with language-specific processing emerging during late-layer transition. Evidence includes: 70--77% within-language probe accuracy, a consistent 6.3--7.9pp directional asymmetry favoring X->EN over EN->X transfer, SAE features showing mid-layer cross-lingual sharing (Jaccard=0.28) that decays in late layers (Jaccard=0.12), and steering flip rates of 17--35% that reproduce the same asymmetry pattern.\n\n---\n\n### Strengths\n\n**S1. Well-structured hypothesis-driven design.** The H1 vs. H2 framing is commendable. Rather than reporting a collection of exploratory findings, the paper derives specific, falsifiable predictions from each hypothesis (mid-layer Jaccard > late-layer Jaccard for H1; directional consistency across language pairs) and systematically tests them. This is good scientific practice rarely seen in interpretability papers.\n\n**S2. Three-track converging evidence.** The detection (probing) -> decomposition (SAE) -> intervention (steering) pipeline provides increasingly strong evidence for the claims. The steering experiments are particularly valuable because they move beyond correlational probing to causal manipulation. The fact that steering flip rate asymmetry mirrors the probe transfer asymmetry (Section 4.5, Tables 6 and 7) is the paper's strongest methodological contribution.\n\n**S3. Systematic confound controls.** The COMET tercile ablation (Table 1) is well-designed: rather than applying an arbitrary COMET threshold, the authors empirically test whether translation quality correlates with probe accuracy and find a null/reversed relationship. The token overlap analysis (Jaccard 3-4%, negative correlation in Llama) is similarly careful. The label noise ablation establishes safety margins relative to the verification pipeline's estimated error rate.\n\n**S4. Cross-architecture consistency.** Testing three architectures and finding a coefficient of variation below 6% for the asymmetry gap (7.2/7.2/7.9pp) is notable. The MLP non-linearity gradient anti-correlating with Chinese pre-training proportion (Table 3: Qwen +2.3pp, Llama +4.7pp, Gemma +6.1pp) is a clean finding that connects model training characteristics to mechanistic behavior.\n\n**S5. Appropriate statistical methodology.** Permutation tests (n=1000) are appropriate for the non-parametric setting. The use of balanced accuracy rather than raw accuracy is correct given the potential for class imbalance. The 5-fold stratified cross-validation is standard and appropriate.\n\n---\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n**W1 (Major). The paper is a \"dream report\" -- all experimental results beyond the MVP are imagined, not executed.**\n\nThis is the single most critical issue. Cross-referencing the paper's tables against the project's actual experimental state reveals a fundamental disconnect:\n\n- The ROADMAP.md shows only 2 experiments completed (`mvp_probe_v1`, `mvp_probe_llama_v1`), both on EN--ZH only, both on Llama-3.1-8B and Qwen3-8B only.\n- The experiment_plan.yaml shows all Post-MVP experiments (plan_102 through plan_303) in `planned` status, not `done`.\n- The `review_context.yaml` and `imagined_experiments.yaml` files explicitly describe these as imagined results in a \"best tier\" scenario.\n- The Gemma-2-9B-IT results (Table 2, row 3) are imagined -- the model download was interrupted at 11% (mistakes.yaml M010).\n- All EN--JA results (Table 4) are imagined -- plan_103 is `planned`.\n- All SAE results (Tables 5, 6, 7, 8, 9) are imagined -- plan_201/202/203/301/302/303 are all `planned`.\n- All steering results (Tables 6, 7, 8) are imagined -- plan_107/301/302 are all `planned`.\n- The CKA/SVCCA results (Table 5) are imagined -- plan_104 is `planned`.\n- The larger dataset validation (Table 10) is imagined -- plan_108 is `planned`.\n- Even the COMET results for Llama are still running (`comet_llama_v6` in report.md).\n\nOnly the core 2-model EN--ZH probe results and the Qwen COMET tercile ablation have been actually executed. Everything else in this paper is speculative. **As a reviewer, I cannot evaluate the soundness of experiments that have not been run.** The numbers could turn out quite differently in practice. The paper should be resubmitted when the experiments are actually completed.\n\n**W2 (Major). Bootstrap n=100 is insufficient for reliable 95% CIs.**\n\nThe standard recommendation for bootstrap CIs is n >= 1000 (Efron & Tibshirani, 1993; DiCiccio & Efron, 1996). At n=100, the 2.5th and 97.5th percentiles of the bootstrap distribution correspond to the 2nd and 3rd most extreme values, making CI endpoints highly sensitive to individual bootstrap samples. This is particularly concerning because:\n\n- The Qwen CI at N~1000 brackets zero ([-1.2, 13.4]), and the paper interprets this as \"trending\" rather than non-significant. With n=100 bootstrap, the lower CI bound could easily shift by 2-3pp either way.\n- The paper claims \"98/100 iterations positive\" for Llama and \"96/100 iterations positive\" for Qwen. These counts have binomial SE of ~1.4% and ~2.0% respectively, which is non-negligible.\n- The asymmetry gap is the paper's central quantitative claim. Using only 100 bootstrap iterations for the key statistical test undermines confidence in the precise CI bounds reported.\n\nThe authors should increase to n >= 2000 bootstrap iterations. This is computationally cheap given that each iteration only retrains a logistic regression probe.\n\n**W3 (Major). SAE analysis on base model vs. probing on instruct-tuned models.**\n\nThe paper applies Qwen-Scope SAEs (trained on Qwen3-8B-Base) for Track 2 while using instruct-tuned models for Track 1 probing. The paper acknowledges this (Limitations paragraph 3) but dismisses it too quickly with \"cross-validation between tracks partially mitigates.\" This mismatch is more serious than acknowledged because:\n\n- RLHF/instruction tuning substantially reorganizes representations, particularly in layers where the paper claims critical conflict processing occurs (mid-to-late layers). Anthropic's own work on monosemanticity notes that SAE features identified in base models may not correspond to the same features in fine-tuned versions.\n- The Jaccard analysis (Table 7) measures feature overlap in the SAE's native feature space on the base model. Applying these same features to explain instruct-model probe behavior is a logical gap.\n- The steering experiments (Tables 6-9) -- if they were actually run -- would need to be performed on the same model the SAE was trained on (base) OR the paper needs to demonstrate that SAE features transfer meaningfully to the instruct model. Neither is done.\n\n**W4 (Moderate). Probe accuracy of 70-77% is substantially below prior work and limits interpretive claims.**\n\nBoer et al. (2026) achieve 0.96 Macro-F1 for monolingual conflict attribution probing. The 70-77% balanced accuracy reported here represents a meaningful gap. The paper does not adequately explain this gap. Possible explanations include: (a) the cross-lingual setting is inherently harder, (b) the ConflictQA verification pipeline introduces noise, (c) the conflict signal in these models is genuinely weaker. The paper should distinguish between these possibilities because they have different implications for the strength of claims. If (c), the \"conflict detection is encoded in a language-universal representation\" claim is only partially supported -- it may be that only a fraction of conflict information is language-universal while a large portion is encoded in ways the linear probe cannot capture.\n\n**W5 (Moderate). Steering captures only 17-35% of samples, leaving the majority of the mechanism unexplained.**\n\nThe flip rates of 17-35% mean that 65-83% of samples are not affected by the intervention. While the paper correctly notes these rates are 3.4-6.5x above random baseline, the absolute magnitude raises questions about what fraction of the conflict resolution mechanism is captured by the linear/sparse approximation. The paper would benefit from an analysis of *which* samples flip and which do not -- are the non-flipping samples systematically different (e.g., higher confidence, particular topic domains)?\n\n**W6 (Moderate). Only two language pairs, both Asian, tested.**\n\nBoth EN--ZH and EN--JA are CJK languages with shared cultural context. The \"English-anchored\" hypothesis would be much more convincing with at least one typologically distant Indo-European language (e.g., EN--DE or EN--FR) where the English anchoring might behave differently due to shared script and morphological similarity. The paper's own framing references CLEAR's 10-language evaluation, making the 2-language coverage feel thin.\n\n**W7 (Minor). No train/test accuracy gap analysis for MLP probes.**\n\nThe paper notes MLP probes achieve +2.3 to +6.1pp gains in the weak transfer direction (Table 3) but does not report train accuracy for MLP probes. With a single hidden layer of 256 units operating on ~1000 samples with dimensionality 3584-4096, there is a real risk of overfitting. The paper states \"MLP probes (single hidden layer, 256 units) are trained with identical splits to quantify non-linear components\" but does not mention regularization (dropout, weight decay). A train/test accuracy comparison is needed to rule out that the MLP gain is simply memorization.\n\n**W8 (Minor). The Jaccard permutation test p=0.008 is based on very small feature counts.**\n\nThe mid-layer Jaccard of 0.28 comes from 8 shared features out of 21 EN and 18 ZH features (Table 7). The late-layer Jaccard of 0.12 comes from 3 shared features out of 14 EN and 10 ZH features. With such small counts, a single feature being reclassified between layers could meaningfully shift the Jaccard index. While p=0.008 passes conventional thresholds, the practical robustness of this finding to feature selection thresholds should be tested via sensitivity analysis.\n\n---\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n**Q1.** Can you confirm which experimental results in the paper have been actually executed vs. projected from the \"dream report\" pipeline? The discrepancy between the experiment tracking system and the paper is concerning.\n\n**Q2.** Why was bootstrap n set to 100 rather than the conventional 1000+? Was this a computational constraint? Given that each bootstrap iteration is a logistic regression fit, this seems unnecessarily conservative.\n\n**Q3.** For the COMET tercile ablation (Table 1), the Qwen tercile sizes are only 56 samples each. Have you computed the statistical power of Fisher's exact test at this sample size to detect a meaningful accuracy difference (say, 10pp)?\n\n**Q4.** The paper reports \"coherence is preserved at 91%\" for probe-based steering (Section 4.5). How is coherence measured? Is this human evaluation, automated metric, or perplexity-based?\n\n**Q5.** In the feature-set ablation (Table 9), \"shared features alone achieve 28.0% cross-lingual flip rate\" vs. EN-only 11.0% and ZH-only 9.0%. Have you verified that this difference is not simply due to shared features being at more causally effective layers (mid-layers) while language-specific features skew late (Table 6)?\n\n**Q6.** The MLP non-linearity gradient (Qwen 2.3pp < Llama 4.7pp < Gemma 6.1pp) is interpreted as anti-correlating with Chinese pre-training proportion. But Table 3 shows Gemma at 6.1pp, which is *higher* than Llama at 4.7pp. Yet Gemma is from Google (strong multilingual training). What evidence do you have about the relative Chinese data proportions in these three models' training sets?\n\n**Q7.** The token overlap analysis found Llama has a *negative* correlation (r=-0.15, p=0.012) between token overlap and probe accuracy. This is unexpected and warrants explanation. Why would higher token overlap *reduce* accuracy?\n\n---\n\n### Minor Issues\n\n1. **Table 2**: \"Sig.\" column uses \"Yes\"/\"No (trend)\" -- a binary significance judgment is more appropriate for a methods paper. The \"trend\" language invites selective interpretation. Report the CI and let readers judge.\n\n2. **Section 3.1**: \"We obtain model responses in English (EN) and generate high-quality translations in Chinese (ZH) and Japanese (JA) using the target models themselves.\" Using the same model for translation and as the probe target introduces a potential circularity -- the model's own biases in translation could systematically affect the conflict patterns in the translated text.\n\n3. **References**: Only 8 references cited in the paper. For an ACL submission, this is thin. Key missing references include: the original ConflictQA paper's full citation (Xie et al., 2024 is cited but only in references, not in the methods section where the dataset is described), Belinkov (2022) for probing methodology best practices, and Raghu et al. (2017) for SVCCA.\n\n4. **Reproducibility**: The paper does not mention random seeds, hyperparameter selection for L2 regularization in logistic regression, or the steering alpha search range/granularity.\n\n5. **Section 4.3**: \"The probe accuracy peak correlates negatively with the CKA transition point (r = -0.71)\" -- with only 3 data points (one per model), this correlation is not meaningful and should not be reported as if it were a statistical finding.\n\n6. The paper uses \"balanced accuracy\" throughout but the actual implementation in `train_probe.py` uses `clf.score()`, which returns raw accuracy, not balanced accuracy. This is a discrepancy between the paper's claims and the code.\n\n---\n\n### Preliminary Scores\n\n- **Soundness**: 2/5 -- The fundamental issue is that the vast majority of reported results are imagined rather than actually executed. The statistical methodology for the actually-completed experiments is generally appropriate but has the n=100 bootstrap weakness. The base/instruct SAE mismatch is inadequately addressed.\n\n- **Overall Assessment**: 2.0/5 (Reject) -- The paper presents an ambitious and well-structured research program with a clean hypothesis-driven design. However, the results as presented are largely speculative projections from a \"dream report\" pipeline rather than actual experimental outcomes. Only the 2-model EN--ZH probe experiments and partial COMET validation have been completed. This paper should be resubmitted once the experiments are actually executed, as the results could differ substantially from projections. If all experiments were genuinely completed and the numbers held, this would be a solid 3.5-4.0 paper. The three-track converging evidence design is strong, the confound controls are thorough, and the hypothesis framework is clean. But science requires actual data.\n\n**Confidence**: 4/5 -- I have high confidence in this assessment. I cross-referenced the paper's claims against the project's experiment tracking system, source code, and decision logs, which clearly distinguish completed from planned experiments.\n\n---\n\n### Actionable Recommendations for Revision\n\n1. Execute all planned experiments and resubmit with actual results.\n2. Increase bootstrap iterations to n >= 2000.\n3. Address the base/instruct SAE mismatch by either running SAE analysis on instruct models or providing transfer validation.\n4. Add at least one non-CJK language pair (e.g., EN--DE).\n5. Report MLP probe train/test accuracy gaps to rule out overfitting.\n6. Fix the `clf.score()` vs. balanced accuracy discrepancy in the code.\n7. Conduct sensitivity analysis on the SAE feature selection threshold and its impact on Jaccard indices."}],"totalDurationMs":157803,"totalTokens":100447,"totalToolUseCount":22,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6301,"cache_read_input_tokens":90053,"output_tokens":4092,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6301},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":4092,"cache_read_input_tokens":90053,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6301,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6301,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":8,"searchCount":4,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":10}}}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"a5ba6c5044de413be","tool_use_id":"toolu_01K1yU3KRea1dw94pQzszMvB","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","usage":{"total_tokens":101329,"tool_uses":24,"duration_ms":173704},"last_tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","uuid":"66af1b62-11a2-4678-82ba-1a23cb68e8e5","session_id":"d76881f3-8cf2-45f7-8657-e72190e5783a"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01MhU91NCnqWUuMHuChUhMha","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01QSUG8Uy8eAoLTpVeNDewFc","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"project":"clkc_mech_interp","tier":"best","dream_id":"dream_20260522T023801","overall_score":"borderline accept","report":"# Reviewer 2 (R2): Novelty, Significance, and Related Work\n\n## Summary\n\nThis paper investigates how cross-lingual knowledge conflict signals are mechanistically encoded in the residual stream of multilingual transformers. Using three complementary analysis tracks—linear/MLP probing, sparse autoencoder (SAE) feature decomposition via Qwen-Scope, and causal representation steering—the authors study three 8-9B parameter models (Llama-3.1-8B, Qwen3-8B, Gemma-2-9B-IT) across two language pairs (EN-ZH, EN-JA). The central finding is a consistent directional asymmetry in cross-lingual conflict signal transfer: X→EN outperforms EN→X by 6.3-7.9pp across all model×language-pair combinations. SAE analysis reveals mid-layer cross-lingual feature sharing (Jaccard=0.28) that diminishes in late layers (0.12), and steering experiments provide causal confirmation. The authors argue this supports an \"English-anchored\" hypothesis (H1) where conflict detection is language-universal and anchored to English representations, while language-specific processing emerges during late-layer transition.\n\n---\n\n## Strengths\n\n**S1. Novel research question at a productive intersection.** This is, to my knowledge, the first mechanistic interpretability study focused specifically on *cross-lingual* knowledge conflict signals. While monolingual conflict probing exists (Zhao et al. 2024; Boer et al. 2026) and cross-lingual representation structure is well-studied (Wendler et al. 2024; Wang et al. 2025), the intersection—how conflict-specific signals interact with multilingual representation geometry—is genuinely unexplored. This positions the work at a novel and productive junction between two active research communities.\n\n**S2. Three-track convergence is methodologically compelling.** The probing → SAE decomposition → causal steering pipeline is well-designed. Each track addresses a different level of analysis (statistical detection → feature-level decomposition → causal intervention), and the convergence of all three on the same directional asymmetry pattern substantially strengthens the claims beyond what any single track could support. The feature-set ablation experiment (Table 11, shared features: 28% flip rate vs. language-specific: 9-11%) is particularly elegant as a fine-grained causal test of H1.\n\n**S3. Rigorous confound control.** The paper systematically addresses the most obvious confounds: translation quality (COMET tercile ablation showing no positive correlation), token overlap (per-sample Jaccard 3-4%, negative correlation with accuracy), and label noise robustness. The LangFIR deconfounding of conflict vs. language-identity features (4.3% overlap) is a thoughtful control that directly addresses the concern that probes might detect language identity rather than conflict signals.\n\n**S4. Remarkable cross-architecture consistency.** The asymmetry gap of +7.2pp / +7.2pp / +7.9pp across three architectures (CV < 6%) is striking. This level of quantitative consistency across independently trained models with different tokenizers, training data mixtures, and architectural details (GQA configurations, sliding windows) lends strong credibility to the finding being a fundamental property of how multilingual transformers encode conflicts rather than a model-specific artifact.\n\n**S5. The English-anchored hypothesis is theoretically meaningful.** While the observation that English dominates internal representations is not entirely surprising given English-heavy pre-training, the specific claim that *conflict detection* (not just general semantics) is English-anchored, and that this anchoring creates measurable directional asymmetries in transfer, is a substantive theoretical contribution. The distinction between detection (language-universal, mid-layer) and resolution (language-specific, late-layer) is well-articulated and testable.\n\n---\n\n## Weaknesses\n\n**W1. The \"English-anchored\" finding may be a trivial consequence of English-dominant pre-training (Major).** The central hypothesis H1—that conflict signals are anchored to English—is presented as a discovery about the *mechanism* of conflict encoding. However, an equally parsimonious explanation is that these models simply have better English representations overall (because 60-80% of pre-training data is English), and *any* semantic task would show the same X→EN > EN→X asymmetry pattern. The paper does not control for this. A critical missing experiment is to test the same asymmetry on a *non-conflict* semantic task (e.g., sentiment detection, NER, topic classification) using the same probing methodology. If the same ~7pp asymmetry appears for arbitrary semantic tasks, the \"English-anchored conflict detection\" narrative collapses into \"English-anchored everything,\" which is already known from Wendler et al. (2024). The authors should demonstrate that the asymmetry is *specific to conflict signals* or at minimum differs in magnitude from non-conflict semantic tasks.\n\n**W2. H1 vs. H2 is not a fair test (Major).** H2 (language-pair-specific encoding) predicts that \"transfer patterns differ qualitatively across language pairs.\" But the paper only tests two East Asian languages paired with English. Both ZH and JA are typologically distant from English, both use CJK scripts (JA partially), and both are high-resource in all three models' training data. Testing H2 requires genuinely typologically diverse pairs—including an Indo-European pair (EN-DE or EN-FR), a low-resource pair (EN-SW or EN-YO), and ideally a non-EN pair (ZH-JA or DE-FR). With only EN-ZH and EN-JA, the experiment cannot distinguish \"English-anchored universal\" from \"both Asian languages happen to behave similarly because they occupy similar positions in these models' representation spaces.\" The claim to have \"tested H1 vs. H2\" is overclaimed given this limitation.\n\n**W3. Reference list is severely inadequate for ACL (Major).** The paper cites only 8 references. A typical ACL paper in this area would cite 30-50. Critical missing areas include:\n\n- **Cross-lingual probing literature**: Pires et al. (2019, \"How multilingual is multilingual BERT?\"), Conneau et al. (2020, \"Emerging cross-lingual structure in pretrained language representations\"), Chi et al. (2020, \"Finding universal grammatical relations in multilingual BERT\"), K et al. (2020, \"Cross-lingual ability of multilingual BERT\"). These are foundational works for any cross-lingual probing study.\n- **Cross-lingual representation analysis**: Muller et al. (2021, \"First align, then predict\"), Libovicky et al. (2020, \"Language through a prism\"), Stanczak et al. (2022). The CKA/SVCCA analysis in Section 4.3 should cite the original cross-lingual representation similarity literature.\n- **Knowledge conflict probing**: Zhao et al. (2024, NeurIPS FMI Workshop, \"Analysing the residual stream\"), Boer et al. (2026, \"Probing for knowledge attribution\"), Pham et al. (2026, \"Where knowledge collides\"). These are listed in the project's own literature_map.yaml but not cited in the paper.\n- **Cross-lingual SAE work**: Kialy et al. (2026, \"A Universal Vibe?\") on cross-lingual SAE feature discovery is a direct predecessor that must be cited. Wong et al. (2026, LangFIR) is used methodologically but not cited.\n- **Multilingual knowledge editing**: Wang et al. (2025, \"Lost in Multilinguality\") is referenced in the text as Wendler et al. but the actual citation appears to be conflated.\n- **Language-specific neurons**: Tang et al. (2024), Kojima et al. (2024), and other work on concept/language neurons in multilingual models.\n\nThe sparse citation count will be noticed immediately by any reviewer and signals either rushed writing or unfamiliarity with the broader literature.\n\n**W4. SAE analysis is limited to a single model on the base variant (Moderate).** Track 2 (SAE) and Track 3 (SAE steering) are conducted only on Qwen3-8B-Base using Qwen-Scope SAEs, while Track 1 probing uses instruct-tuned models. This creates two concerns: (a) the SAE results may not generalize across architectures (the paper's strongest claim is cross-architecture consistency, but Track 2/3 are single-model), and (b) the base-vs-instruct mismatch means the three tracks are not analyzing the same model, weakening the \"convergence\" argument. The paper acknowledges this in limitations but does not adequately address why the three tracks should converge despite analyzing different model variants.\n\n**W5. Absolute probe accuracies are modest (Moderate).** Within-language balanced accuracies of 70-77% are substantially below the 90%+ reported by Zhao et al. (2024) for monolingual conflict probing and the 96% F1 by Boer et al. (2026). Cross-lingual transfer at 60-73% is only 10-23pp above chance. While statistically significant, these modest accuracies raise questions about whether the probes are detecting the primary conflict signal or a weak correlate. The paper should discuss why its accuracies are lower than prior monolingual work—is it the cross-lingual setting, the dataset (ConflictQA vs. custom datasets), or the model sizes?\n\n**W6. 13 tables in 8 pages creates density problems (Minor-Moderate).** The paper contains 13 tables (Tables 1-13) within the main text. Several could be consolidated or moved to appendix: the label noise ablation (Table 13), sample size scaling (Table 12), and the typological correlation (discussed only in text). The current density makes the paper feel like a technical report rather than a narrative-driven conference paper. The three-track structure, while logical, compounds this by requiring separate result sections for each track.\n\n---\n\n## Questions for Authors\n\n**Q1.** Have you tested whether the ~7pp X→EN > EN→X asymmetry also appears for non-conflict semantic tasks (e.g., sentiment, topic) using the same probing methodology? If so, what is the magnitude comparison? This is essential for ruling out the explanation that \"English-anchored\" is simply \"better English representations\" rather than a conflict-specific phenomenon.\n\n**Q2.** The MLP non-linearity gain anti-correlates with Chinese pre-training data proportion (Qwen 2.3pp < Llama 4.7pp < Gemma 6.1pp). But you claim Gemma has *weaker* ZH alignment than Llama—what is the evidence for this ordering? The claim appears to be post-hoc narrative fitting without independent verification of each model's ZH training data proportions.\n\n**Q3.** Why are your within-language probe accuracies (70-77%) substantially lower than prior monolingual work (~90-96%)? Is this an inherent property of the ConflictQA dataset, the models, or your probing methodology? What happens with a 2-layer MLP probe rather than single-hidden-layer?\n\n**Q4.** For the SAE analysis: how sensitive are the 47 identified conflict features to the MI threshold and filtering criteria? What does the feature count and Jaccard look like at different thresholds (e.g., top-64 vs. top-256 candidates)?\n\n**Q5.** The probe accuracy peak correlates with the CKA transition point (r=-0.71), but this is computed across only 3 data points (3 models). A correlation from n=3 is not statistically meaningful. How do you justify reporting this?\n\n**Q6.** For steering experiments: how was the steering coefficient alpha=2.5 selected? Was it tuned on a held-out set or on the same evaluation data? What is the flip rate curve as a function of alpha?\n\n---\n\n## Minor Issues\n\n**M1.** The paper title says \"Mechanistic Evidence\" but the probing and CKA analyses are correlational, not mechanistic in the strict sense (causal intervention on model components). Only Track 3 steering provides mechanistic/causal evidence. Consider softening to \"Evidence from Probing, Feature Decomposition, and Steering\" or similar.\n\n**M2.** The Gao et al. (2024) citation for SpARE appears to be the same group as Zhao et al. (2025) who published SpARE at NAACL 2025. The bibliography should be checked for accuracy—the literature map lists Zhao et al. (2025) as the SpARE paper, not Gao et al.\n\n**M3.** Wendler et al. (2024) is cited for \"language transition\" but the actual paper title is \"Do Llamas Work in English?\" The text references \"Lost in Multilinguality\" which appears to be Wang et al. (2025), a different paper. This conflation should be resolved.\n\n**M4.** The \"first mechanistic investigation\" claim in the Conclusion should be qualified. Pham et al. (2026, \"Where Knowledge Collides\") use activation patching for intra-memory conflicts, which is arguably mechanistic investigation of knowledge conflicts. The \"first *cross-lingual* mechanistic investigation\" would be more defensible.\n\n**M5.** Table 4 (multilingual asymmetry) repeats the EN-ZH rows from Table 2. Consider integrating into a single table.\n\n**M6.** The paper uses \"ConflictQA\" throughout but does not cite the original ConflictQA paper (Xie et al. 2024 appears to be the citation, but it is described as \"Adaptive chameleon or stubborn sloth\" which may be a different paper). Verify the correct citation.\n\n**M7.** CKA (Kornblith et al. 2019) is used but SVCCA is mentioned without citation. Raghu et al. (2017) should be cited.\n\n---\n\n## Assessment of Specific Claims\n\n| Claim | Assessment |\n|-------|-----------|\n| \"First mechanistic investigation of cross-lingual conflict signals\" | **Likely true** but should be qualified (Pham et al. 2026 do mechanistic conflict analysis, though monolingual). Cross-lingual specificity is the novel aspect. |\n| H1 supported over H2 | **Partially supported** but the test is underpowered. Two East Asian language pairs cannot distinguish \"English-anchored universal\" from \"similar behavior for typologically similar languages.\" |\n| Three-track convergence | **Genuine strength**, though weakened by the base/instruct mismatch and single-model SAE analysis. |\n| \"English-anchored\" as a general principle | **Overclaimed** without non-conflict baselines. May be trivially true for all semantic tasks given English-dominant training. |\n| Asymmetry is cross-architecture robust | **Well-supported** by the quantitative consistency (CV < 6%) across 3 architectures. |\n| SAE conflict features are distinct from language-identity features | **Reasonably supported** by LangFIR control (4.3% overlap, different layer distributions). |\n\n---\n\n## Significance and Impact Assessment\n\n**Who benefits?** Primarily the mechanistic interpretability community (new domain: cross-lingual conflict signals) and secondarily the multilingual NLP community (understanding why linguistic affinity matters for conflict resolution). The English-anchored hypothesis, if validated with stronger controls, could influence how multilingual models are trained and evaluated.\n\n**Practical implications.** The steering results suggest that cross-lingual conflict resolution could be manipulated at inference time without language-specific fine-tuning, which has practical value for multilingual deployment. The 69% transfer efficiency provides a quantitative baseline for this capability.\n\n**Venue appropriateness.** The work is at the intersection of mechanistic interpretability and multilingual NLP, making it appropriate for ACL/EMNLP/ICLR. The three-track methodology is ambitious and the experimental scope (3 models, 2 language pairs, 3 analysis methods) is substantial. However, the thin related work section and the missing non-conflict baseline experiment are significant weaknesses for a top venue.\n\n**Does three-track convergence strengthen or repeat?** It genuinely strengthens. Each track answers a different question: \"Is the signal detectable?\" (probing), \"What features encode it?\" (SAE), \"Does it causally matter?\" (steering). The asymmetry pattern appearing in all three is more than repetition—it's triangulation.\n\n---\n\n## Preliminary Scores\n\n- **Excitement**: 3.5/5 — The research question is novel and the three-track methodology is well-conceived. The consistent cross-architecture asymmetry is a genuine finding. However, the \"English-anchored\" interpretation may be a trivial consequence of training data composition rather than a deep mechanistic insight, and this concern significantly dampens excitement until non-conflict baselines are provided.\n\n- **Overall Assessment**: 3.5/5 (borderline accept) — Strong experimental design and a novel research question at a productive intersection. The three-track convergence and cross-architecture consistency are genuine contributions. However, three issues prevent a clear accept: (1) the missing non-conflict baseline that could deflate the central claim, (2) the severely thin related work section, and (3) the underpowered H1-vs-H2 test using only two East Asian language pairs. If the authors can address W1 (non-conflict baseline) and W3 (references) in revision, this would move to a clear accept.\n\n---\n\n## Actionable Recommendations (Ranked by Priority)\n\n1. **[Critical]** Add a non-conflict semantic task baseline to demonstrate that the ~7pp asymmetry is conflict-specific rather than a general property of English-dominant representations.\n2. **[Critical]** Expand the reference list to 30+ papers covering cross-lingual probing, multilingual representation analysis, knowledge conflict mechanisms, and cross-lingual SAE work. The current 8 references are unacceptable for any top venue.\n3. **[High]** Add at least one non-CJK language pair (EN-DE or EN-FR) to strengthen the H1-vs-H2 test. Two East Asian languages do not constitute typological diversity.\n4. **[High]** Discuss why within-language probe accuracies (70-77%) are substantially below prior monolingual baselines (90-96%).\n5. **[Medium]** Consolidate tables—move label noise ablation and sample size scaling to appendix. Aim for 8-9 main text tables maximum.\n6. **[Medium]** Fix bibliography errors (SpARE attribution, Wendler/Wang conflation, missing SVCCA citation, missing LangFIR citation).\n7. **[Low]** Qualify the r=-0.71 CKA-probe correlation as illustrative rather than statistically meaningful (n=3).\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":761,"cache_read_input_tokens":99703,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":761,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01K1yU3KRea1dw94pQzszMvB","session_id":"d76881f3-8cf2-45f7-8657-e72190e5783a","uuid":"6a0a6bec-f3d6-417c-8001-b39acd3a2e30"}
