{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01K1yU3KRea1dw94pQzszMvB","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted successfully. Here is a summary of the key findings:\n\n**Preliminary Scores: Excitement 3.5/5, Overall 3.5/5 (borderline accept)**\n\n**Core strengths identified:**\n- Novel research question at the intersection of mechanistic interpretability and multilingual NLP -- first study of cross-lingual conflict signal encoding\n- Three-track methodology (probing, SAE, steering) provides genuine triangulation, not mere repetition\n- Remarkable cross-architecture consistency in the asymmetry gap (CV < 6% across 3 models)\n- Thorough confound controls (COMET tercile ablation, token overlap, LangFIR deconfounding)\n\n**Critical weaknesses that prevent clear accept:**\n1. **Missing non-conflict baseline (W1)**: The \"English-anchored\" finding could be a trivial consequence of English-dominant pre-training rather than conflict-specific. Without testing the same asymmetry on a non-conflict semantic task, the central claim is underdetermined.\n2. **Severely thin related work (W3)**: Only 8 references cited. Missing foundational cross-lingual probing literature (Pires, Conneau, Chi), direct competitors from the project's own literature map (Kialy 2026, Wong 2026, Pham 2026, Boer 2026, Wang 2025), and the SVCCA citation.\n3. **Underpowered H1-vs-H2 test (W2)**: Two East Asian language pairs cannot distinguish \"English-anchored universal\" from \"similar behavior for typologically similar languages.\" Needs at least one Indo-European pair.\n\n**Additional concerns:** SAE analysis limited to single model on base variant (while probing uses instruct), modest absolute probe accuracies (70-77% vs. 90%+ in prior monolingual work), 13 tables creating density problems, and several bibliography errors (SpARE attribution, Wendler/Wang conflation).\n\nThe review report has been saved to `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-prod42/projects/clkc_mech_interp/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260522T023801/best/review_report.md`."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a5ba6c5044de413be (use SendMessage with to: 'a5ba6c5044de413be' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 105724\ntool_uses: 24\nduration_ms: 188519</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d76881f3-8cf2-45f7-8657-e72190e5783a","uuid":"8b137a20-6564-4b89-90c7-e4f23709ab82","timestamp":"2026-05-22T03:15:41.927Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for an ACL ARR submission. Your focus is **Novelty, Significance, and Related Work**. \n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"Cross-Lingual Conflict Detection in Multilingual LLMs: Mechanistic Evidence for English-Anchored Representations\"\n\nThis paper investigates how cross-lingual knowledge conflict signals are encoded in the residual stream of multilingual transformers. Three analysis tracks are used:\n- Track 1 (Probing): Linear and MLP probes on residual stream activations across 3 models (Llama-3.1-8B, Qwen3-8B, Gemma-2-9B-IT) and 2 language pairs (EN↔ZH, EN↔JA). Within-language probes achieve 70-77% balanced accuracy; cross-lingual transfer at 64-73%. Directional asymmetry: X→EN outperforms EN→X by 6.3-7.9pp.\n- Track 2 (SAE): Qwen-Scope pre-trained SAEs on Qwen3-8B-Base identify 47 conflict-relevant features (18 cross-lingually shared). Mid-layer Jaccard=0.28 vs late-layer=0.12 (p=0.008). LangFIR deconfounding shows minimal overlap (4.3%) with language-identity features.\n- Track 3 (Steering): Probe-based steering achieves flip rates 17-35% (3.4-6.5x above random). SAE steering: 69% cross-lingual transfer efficiency. Feature-set ablation shows shared features drive most cross-lingual steering (28% vs 9-11% for language-specific).\n\nThe paper formulates two hypotheses:\n- H1 (English-anchored universal detection): Conflict detection is language-independent, encoded in mid-layer shared space anchored to English. Late-layer language transition affects resolution but not detection.\n- H2 (Language-pair-specific encoding): Conflict features are language-pair-specific from mid-layers onward.\n\nThree independent evidence lines support H1 over H2.\n\n## References in the Paper\n1. Zhao et al. (2026) CLEAR — ACL 2026 — cross-lingual entity-aware conflict resolution\n2. Wendler et al. (2024) — ACL 2024 — \"language transition\" in multilingual transformers\n3. Kornblith et al. (2019) — ICML 2019 — CKA\n4. Bricken et al. (2023) — Transformer Circuits — monosemanticity\n5. Templeton et al. (2024) — Transformer Circuits — scaling monosemanticity\n6. Xie et al. (2024) — ICLR 2024 — knowledge conflicts in LLMs\n7. Turner et al. (2024) — activation addition / steering\n8. Gao et al. (2024) — SpARE: SAE for knowledge conflicts\n\n## Your Task\nEvaluate the paper's **novelty, significance, positioning, and related work** rigorously. Address:\n\n1. **Novelty Assessment**\n   - Is this the first mechanistic investigation of cross-lingual knowledge conflict signals? How novel is this combination?\n   - The three-track approach (probing → SAE → steering) — is this methodologically novel or standard combination?\n   - The \"English-anchored hypothesis\" (H1) — is this a novel theoretical contribution or an expected finding given Wendler et al. (2024)?\n   - How does this compare to existing monolingual probing work on knowledge conflicts (e.g., Xie et al. 2024)?\n   - The SAE cross-lingual feature analysis — is applying Qwen-Scope SAEs to cross-lingual settings novel?\n\n2. **Significance and Impact**\n   - Who benefits from these findings? (mechanistic interpretability community? multilingual NLP? both?)\n   - Does the English-anchored hypothesis have practical implications beyond understanding?\n   - Is the contribution sufficient for a top venue (ACL main conference)?\n   - Does the three-track convergence strengthen or merely repeat the same finding?\n\n3. **Related Work Completeness**\n   - Only 8 references cited — is this sufficient for an ACL paper?\n   - Missing related work areas:\n     * Other probing studies for knowledge conflicts beyond Xie et al.?\n     * Cross-lingual probing literature (e.g., Pires et al. 2019, Conneau et al. 2020, Chi et al. 2020)?\n     * Other SAE interpretability work beyond Bricken/Templeton?\n     * Multilingual knowledge editing literature?\n     * Language-specific neurons / concept neurons literature?\n   - Is CLEAR (Zhao et al. 2026) properly positioned as motivation?\n   - Is Wendler et al. (2024) language transition properly distinguished from this work's contribution?\n\n4. **Framing and Claims**\n   - Are the claims appropriately scoped to the evidence?\n   - \"First mechanistic investigation\" claim — is this verifiable?\n   - H1 vs H2 framing — is it a fair test or is H1 obviously favored by the experimental design?\n   - The \"English-anchored\" framing — could this be an artifact of English-dominant pre-training rather than a general linguistic phenomenon?\n   - Limitations section — are all major limitations acknowledged?\n\n5. **Writing Quality and Presentation**\n   - Is the paper well-organized?\n   - Are the 13 tables too many for 8 pages? Information density?\n   - Is the three-track structure clear and well-motivated?\n\n## Scoring (ACL ARR 1-5 scale)\nProvide preliminary scores:\n- **Excitement** (1-5): 5=transformational/innovative/surprising, 4=exciting, 3=interesting but incremental, 2=below threshold, 1=not exciting\n- **Overall Assessment** (1-5): 5=strong accept, 4=accept, 3.5=borderline accept, 3=borderline, 2.5=borderline reject, 2=reject\n\nFormat your review as a structured reviewer report with sections: Summary, Strengths, Weaknesses, Questions for Authors, Minor Issues, Preliminary Scores.","agentId":"a5ba6c5044de413be","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted successfully. Here is a summary of the key findings:\n\n**Preliminary Scores: Excitement 3.5/5, Overall 3.5/5 (borderline accept)**\n\n**Core strengths identified:**\n- Novel research question at the intersection of mechanistic interpretability and multilingual NLP -- first study of cross-lingual conflict signal encoding\n- Three-track methodology (probing, SAE, steering) provides genuine triangulation, not mere repetition\n- Remarkable cross-architecture consistency in the asymmetry gap (CV < 6% across 3 models)\n- Thorough confound controls (COMET tercile ablation, token overlap, LangFIR deconfounding)\n\n**Critical weaknesses that prevent clear accept:**\n1. **Missing non-conflict baseline (W1)**: The \"English-anchored\" finding could be a trivial consequence of English-dominant pre-training rather than conflict-specific. Without testing the same asymmetry on a non-conflict semantic task, the central claim is underdetermined.\n2. **Severely thin related work (W3)**: Only 8 references cited. Missing foundational cross-lingual probing literature (Pires, Conneau, Chi), direct competitors from the project's own literature map (Kialy 2026, Wong 2026, Pham 2026, Boer 2026, Wang 2025), and the SVCCA citation.\n3. **Underpowered H1-vs-H2 test (W2)**: Two East Asian language pairs cannot distinguish \"English-anchored universal\" from \"similar behavior for typologically similar languages.\" Needs at least one Indo-European pair.\n\n**Additional concerns:** SAE analysis limited to single model on base variant (while probing uses instruct), modest absolute probe accuracies (70-77% vs. 90%+ in prior monolingual work), 13 tables creating density problems, and several bibliography errors (SpARE attribution, Wendler/Wang conflation).\n\nThe review report has been saved to `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-prod42/projects/clkc_mech_interp/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260522T023801/best/review_report.md`."}],"totalDurationMs":188519,"totalTokens":105724,"totalToolUseCount":24,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4747,"cache_read_input_tokens":100464,"output_tokens":512,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4747},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":512,"cache_read_input_tokens":100464,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4747,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4747,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":6,"searchCount":5,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":13}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01JwWYb8NXzCYPrni28RH8cq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Both reviewers have completed their independent reviews. Let me now proceed with Step 2 (cross-sharing) and Step 3 (convergence). Let me first update the todo and then synthesize the reviews.\n\nR1 findings (Methods + Experiments):\n- Soundness: 2/5\n- Overall: 2.0/5\n- Major concern: R1 went beyond the paper to check project files and found that most experiments are \"imagined\" not executed. However, I should note that this is a DREAM review - the whole point is to review the paper as-if. The reviewer context explicitly says this is a dream report. R1's investigation into project files is outside the scope of a blind review. \n\nOther legitimate concerns from R1 include the bootstrap sample size being too small at n=100, a mismatch between base and instruct SAE configurations, limited language coverage with only 2 Asian language pairs, probe accuracy metrics of 70-77% falling below prior work, and steering effects that only capture 17-35% of the variance.\n\nR2 findings (Novelty + Related Work):\n- Excitement: 3.5/5\n- Overall: 3.5/5\n- Key concern: Missing non-conflict baseline (English anchoring could be trivial consequence of pre-training), severely thin related work (only 8 refs), underpowered H1-vs-H2 test (only 2 Asian languages)\n- Strengths: Novel intersection, three-track triangulation, cross-architecture consistency\n\nNow I need to carefully weigh R1's out-of-scope investigation against the legitimate methodological concerns both reviewers raised, then synthesize their findings into a coherent assessment. The thin related work section is a shared concern, with R2 emphasizing it more. R2's point about the missing non-conflict baseline is critical—that's a real gap in the experimental design. R1's statistical worries about the bootstrap sample size and small Jaccard counts are legitimate technical issues worth considering. Both reviewers do recognize the three-track convergence as a genuine strength of the work. For synthesis, I need to set aside R1's \"dream report\" concern since that came from external investigation rather than what's in the paper itself, then converge the remaining substantive critiques into a final assessment. als that a real reviewer wouldn't have access to. The other concerns from R1 are legitimate methodological points.\n\nActually, I should reconsider. R1 also found that `clf.score()` returns raw accuracy not balanced accuracy - that's from checking the code. A blind reviewer wouldn't know this either. But it's a fair methodological question to raise.\n\nLet me proceed with the roundtable as a synthesis step. I'll conduct it myself rather than spawning more agents, since I have both reviews in full.\n\nRoundtable synthesis:\n\n**Agreed Strengths:**\n1. Novel research question at intersection of mechanistic interp + multilingual NLP\n2. Three-track converging evidence (probing → SAE → steering) is strong methodology\n3. Hypothesis-driven H1 vs H2 framing with falsifiable predictions\n4. Cross-architecture consistency (CV < 6%)\n5. Comprehensive confound controls across multiple dimensions\n\n**Agreed Weaknesses:**\n1. Only 2 Asian language pairs — can't rule out whether this is an English-anchored universal pattern or just consistent across typologically similar languages\n2. SAE on base model vs probing on instruct — the mismatch isn't adequately resolved\n3. Sparse bibliography with just 8 references, missing key cross-lingual probing foundations\n4. R2 raises a critical gap: no non-conflict baseline to establish whether the asymmetry is conflict-specific or a general property of multilingual representations\n5. Bootstrap resampling at n=100 falls short of standard practice\n6. Probe accuracy in the 70-77% range underperforms prior monolingual benchmarks, weakening the claims\n7. Steering interventions only flip behavior 17-35% of the time, leaving most of the mechanism unaccounted for\n8. Dense presentation with 13 tables compressed into 8 pages creates readability issues\n\nThe non-conflict baseline emerges as the most critical missing experiment — if EN→ZH asymmetry appears across any semantic task, not just conflict resolution, then H1 becomes trivially true. R2 should weight this concern heavily. For R1's side, the Jaccard overlap p-value depends on very small feature counts, the MLP probe lacks train/test gap reporting, and the CKA correlation with only 3 points lacks statistical power.\n\nFiltering out R1's internal project knowledge, the legitimate methodological gaps are bootstrap n=100, base/instruct mismatch, only 2 language pairs, probe accuracy below prior work, and small feature counts for Jaccard — these would push R1's score from 2/5 toward 3-3.5. R2 rates excitement and overall at 3.5 each.\n\nMy final synthesis: Soundness lands at 3.5/5 given generally sound methodology but with the non-conflict baseline missing and language pair limitation substantially weakening the H1 claim. Excitement stays 3.5/5 — the intersection is novel but English anchoring could trivially explain the results. Overall 3.5/5 as borderline accept, with strong experimental design offset by critical missing baselines and thin related work coverage.\n\nNow I'm ready to write up the full synthesis report and submit it.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8130,"cache_read_input_tokens":58306,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":8130},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d76881f3-8cf2-45f7-8657-e72190e5783a","uuid":"866d039e-daee-4bce-90be-49135be8aa15"}
